      "math_academy_id": "q-332768",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode.",
      "worked_solution": "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221258/assets/1fe96278d09a5875c2f1e7c8762a808fd9549b37508f70b129b20e311b166308.png)",
